// Brought to u by MidManStudio
//
// Docs only, no code: records the allocator discussion of chat 10 in docs/LLVM_ROADMAP.md as decisions D9 to D11 (all marked
// provisional) and points open decision 3 at them. Two anchored edits, each anchor occurs exactly once and neither is a whole line.
// This overwrites the earlier patch.mdix, which is right: the earlier one (the benchmark log entry) is applied on master.

@CONFIG(
  version    -> "1.0.0",
  author     -> "MidManStudio",
  features   -> "quickfuncs,data",
  debug_mode -> "off"
)

@QUICKFUNCS(
  ~patch_replace_text<object>(_path, _anchor, _content) {
    return { path = _path, op = "replace_text", anchor = _anchor, content = _content }
  }
)

@DATA(
  patch_files::
    patch_replace_text("docs/LLVM_ROADMAP.md", "| D8 | Proof assistant, or paper proofs only | B5 | Paper first.", "| D8 | Proof assistant, or paper proofs only | B5 | Paper first. |\n| D9 | Which allocator kinds does MID build into the language? | B2 | DECIDED by the owner (2026-11-24), provisional: the lifetime-shape rule. A kind is built in only if it has a distinct lifetime shape that scope-based reclamation expresses and that neither tracing (HIGH) nor per-object free (LOW) serves: the arena (everything dies together) and the pool (same-size objects with individual lifetimes and stable handles). Combinators (tracking, fallback, size routing, a thread-safe wrapper) and any further kind are std, written in Ubel once traits exist, with the mid-alloc and mid-arena crates of mid-engine as the design to port. A general malloc and free heap is LOW's job, not MID's. Nothing implemented. |\n| D10 | Where does a MID function outside its own `with arena` allocate (open decision 3 in MEMORY_MODEL.md)? | B2, M3c, the arena benchmark row | Leaning (owner, 2026-11-24), not final: a hybrid. The default is a hidden ambient-allocator parameter: a function that allocates, directly or through its callees, receives its caller's innermost open allocator; a call with none open is a compile error; sema tags the result with that region (D1 v2). An explicit named handle comes with it, for the cases where two arenas are live at once (an interner arena that outlives a per-file AST arena). Considered and not preferred so far: a run-time current-arena pointer (the error shows only when the call runs, and async tasks would need it saved per task) and explicit-only (new syntax, the longest wait). First stage proposed, not started: the hidden parameter for arenas only, resolved statically in the emitter. It would also settle every refusal that says where it allocates is not decided yet (structs, enums, strings, and lists in arenas). |\n| D11 | Syntax and variants for MID allocators | B2 | Open. Candidates, placeholders and none decided: the kind in the head, with options for variants of one family, `with arena(500KB)` (grows in chunks) and `with arena(500KB, fixed)` (a hard limit); `with pool<T>(n)` stays a separate form because it is typed and frees individually; `as name` binds an explicit handle and `in name` allocates in it; later `with` over any std allocator value once traits exist. Open too: whether the scratch (reset and reuse) and fixed-limit variants are options on arena or forms of their own, and what a full fixed-limit arena does, since the language has no out-of-memory channel yet. `with gc` and `with heap` are still in the grammar and refused by the emitter (B2 item 4: specify or remove).")
    patch_replace_text("docs/LLVM_ROADMAP.md", "a MID helper could simply not be able to build values for its caller.", "a MID helper could simply not be able to build values for its caller. Chat 10 found that this does not fall out of D1: the arena binary-trees benchmark needs a MID function (`make`) to return a tree built in its caller's arena, which D1 as built (borrowed parameters only) does not cover. The allocator questions are now D9 to D11.")
)
